---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import IphoneMockup from '../../components/IphoneMockup.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import BrowserSupport from '../../components/BrowserSupport.astro';
import ResourceList from '../../components/ResourceList.astro';

const browserRows = [
  { browser: 'Safari iOS', version: 'All', supported: 'partial', notes: '-webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent removes the flash' },
  { browser: 'Chrome iOS', version: 'All', supported: 'partial', notes: 'Same WebKit engine — same behaviour and fix' },
  { browser: 'Chrome Android', version: 'All', supported: 'partial', notes: 'Has its own tap ripple — controlled separately' },
  { browser: 'Firefox', version: 'All', supported: true, notes: 'No tap highlight by default' },
  { browser: 'Safari macOS', version: 'All', supported: true, notes: 'No tap highlight (mouse-driven)' },
];

const resources = [
  {
    title: 'MDN: -webkit-tap-highlight-color',
    url: 'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/-webkit-tap-highlight-color',
    description: 'Reference page for the non-standard CSS property that controls the tap highlight.'
  },
  {
    title: 'CSS-Tricks: iOS tap highlight color',
    url: 'https://css-tricks.com/snippets/css/tap-highlight-color/',
    description: 'Quick snippet and explanation for removing the default iOS tap highlight.'
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Unwanted Tap Highlight Flash"
  summary="Tapping interactive elements on iOS shows a translucent grey highlight flash, which looks broken on custom-styled buttons and links."
  affectedSince="All iOS versions"
  slug="tap-highlight-color"
>
  <style>
    .mockup-center { display: flex; justify-content: flex-start; margin: 2rem 0; }

    .phone-screen {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      background: #111;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      gap: 14px;
      padding: 32px 16px 16px;
    }
    .demo-button {
      width: 100%;
      height: 36px;
      background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0A84FF, #5ac8fa);
      border-radius: 10px;
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
      color: #fff;
      position: relative;
      overflow: hidden;
    }
    .tap-highlight-overlay {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
      border-radius: 10px;
    }
    .demo-button-plain {
      width: 100%;
      height: 36px;
      background: #2a2a2a;
      border: 1px solid #444;
      border-radius: 10px;
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      font-size: 10px;
      color: #ccc;
      position: relative;
      overflow: hidden;
    }
    .highlight-badge {
      position: absolute;
      top: -6px;
      right: -6px;
      background: #ff453a;
      color: #fff;
      font-size: 6px;
      font-weight: 700;
      padding: 2px 5px;
      border-radius: 4px;
    }
    .demo-link {
      font-size: 10px;
      color: var(--accent);
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 4px;
      padding: 4px 8px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      position: relative;
    }
    .link-highlight {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25);
      border-radius: 4px;
    }

    .tip {
      background: rgba(48,209,88,0.07);
      border: 1px solid rgba(48,209,88,0.2);
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      color: #ccc;
      margin: 1rem 0;
      line-height: 1.6;
    }
    .tip strong { color: #30d158; }
  </style>

  <h2>The Problem</h2>

  <p>
    On iOS, every tappable element — buttons, links, anything with a click handler — gets a brief translucent
    grey highlight when tapped. On standard blue links it's mostly unnoticeable, but on custom-styled buttons
    it looks like a dark flash or broken opacity animation.
  </p>

  <div class="mockup-center">
    <IphoneMockup label="Default grey flash on tap">
      <div class="phone-screen">
        <div class="demo-button">
          ADD TO CART
          <div class="tap-highlight-overlay"></div>
          <div class="highlight-badge">TAP FLASH</div>
        </div>
        <div class="demo-button-plain">
          Secondary Action
          <div class="tap-highlight-overlay" style="background:rgba(0,0,0,0.2)"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="demo-link">
          View details
          <div class="link-highlight"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </IphoneMockup>
  </div>

  <h2>Root Cause</h2>

  <p>
    WebKit applies a default <code>-webkit-tap-highlight-color</code> to all clickable elements via its User Agent stylesheet.
    The default value is a semi-transparent grey (<code>rgba(0,0,0,0.18)</code> approximately), which appears
    as a dark flash over the element on tap.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is an intentional iOS UI convention — it provides visual feedback that a tap registered. But it ignores any
    custom <code>:active</code> or <code>:focus</code> styles you've set, creating a double-feedback problem with
    custom-designed components.
  </p>

  <h2>Fix — Remove the highlight, add your own :active</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="css"
    filename="global.css"
    code={`/* Step 1: Remove the default tap highlight globally */
* {
  -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
}

/* Or target only interactive elements */
button,
a,
[role="button"],
[tabindex] {
  -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
}

/* Step 2: Replace with your own :active feedback */
button:active {
  opacity: 0.75;
  transform: scale(0.98);
  transition: transform 0.1s, opacity 0.1s;
}

a:active {
  opacity: 0.7;
}`}
  />

  <div class="tip">
    <strong>Accessibility reminder:</strong> Always replace the tap highlight with <em>some</em> form of interactive
    feedback — <code>:active</code> opacity, background colour shift, or scale. Removing highlight with no replacement
    makes buttons feel unresponsive.
  </div>

  <h2>Custom Highlight Colour</h2>

  <p>
    Instead of removing it entirely, you can set a custom colour — your brand's accent colour at low opacity
    makes tap feedback feel intentional rather than broken.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="css"
    filename="brand.css"
    code={`/* Brand-coloured tap feedback instead of grey */
button,
a {
  -webkit-tap-highlight-color: rgba(10, 132, 255, 0.15);
}`}
  />

  <h2>Browser Support</h2>

  <BrowserSupport rows={browserRows} />

  <h2>Further Reading</h2>

  <ResourceList links={resources} />
</ArticleLayout>
